1a:["$","section",null,{"className":"mt-6 rounded-md border border-border bg-bg-panel p-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold text-fg","children":"The Register Collapse"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"LinkedIn ads that convert use professional-confident language. They name a specific problem, reference a recognisable context, and make a claim that sounds like something a senior peer would say - not something a vendor would broadcast. The tone is direct without being pushy. The specificity signals that the writer understands the professional world the reader lives in."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"Then the click lands. And the page says something like: \"Unlock your potential with our cutting-edge platform.\" Or \"Transform your business with best-in-class solutions.\" The register has collapsed from professional-specific to marketing-generic in a single scroll. The visitor noticed the gap even if they cannot articulate it."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"This is not a copywriting problem in the narrow sense. It is an alignment problem. The ad made an implicit contract - \"I understand your world\" - and the landing page immediately broke it. The visitor no longer trusts that the product will deliver what the ad implied."}]]}]
1b:["$","section",null,{"className":"mt-6 rounded-md border border-border bg-bg-panel p-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold text-fg","children":"The Credibility Erosion Pattern: 5 Things That Undo Your LinkedIn Ad"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"These are not hypothetical risks. They are the specific patterns that appear most frequently when we audit landing pages receiving LinkedIn traffic that fails to convert."}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-fg","children":"1. Stock Photos That Contradict the Ad Tone"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"If your ad uses precise, problem-specific language and your landing page hero shows a smiling person in a generic office, the mismatch is visual before it is verbal. Stock imagery associated with consumer software or lifestyle marketing actively undermines the professional credibility your ad built. B2B buyers are pattern-matching for \"this is for someone like me\" - and a generic hero image fails that test instantly."}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-fg","children":"2. Switching to Casual or Consumer Language"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"Words like \"amazing,\" \"supercharge,\" \"game-changer,\" or \"easy\" read as consumer-grade marketing on a page targeting senior buyers. LinkedIn ad copy tends to avoid these because experienced copywriters know the audience. Landing pages that were built for broad traffic and then pointed at LinkedIn campaigns carry this language by default. Each casual word is a small credibility leak."}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-fg","children":"3. Showing Generic Pricing"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"A LinkedIn ad targeting heads of engineering at 500-person SaaS companies implies a certain price point and a certain buying context. A pricing section that shows \"Starter / Pro / Enterprise\" tiers with no reference to the buyer profile signals that the product was not built for that specific buyer - it was built for anyone. Generic pricing on a professional-audience landing page reads as a mismatch between the targeting precision of the ad and the positioning maturity of the product."}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-fg","children":"4. Burying B2B-Specific Proof Below the Fold"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"Case studies from companies your visitor recognises, metrics from businesses at their scale, testimonials from people with their title - these are the proof types that convert professional LinkedIn traffic. Pages built for general SEO traffic often bury this proof in a lower section while using the hero and first section for feature lists or benefit statements. For LinkedIn visitors, the proof needs to arrive before the pitch, not after."}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-fg","children":"5. A Form That Asks for a Personal Email on a Professional Offer"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"\"Enter your email\" with no qualification - or worse, a form that accepts Gmail and Hotmail addresses on a product marketed to B2B teams - signals that the offer is not actually designed for professional buyers. Asking for a work email explicitly, or requiring company size or role, does two things: it filters for qualified leads and it signals to the visitor that this product is built for professional contexts. The form is the last thing standing between the visit and the conversion. It should reinforce professional seriousness, not erode it."}]]}]
1c:["$","section",null,{"className":"mt-6 rounded-md border border-border bg-bg-panel p-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold text-fg","children":"Why LinkedIn-Specific Landing Pages Outperform Generic Ones by 2–3x"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"The 2–3x conversion lift from LinkedIn-specific landing pages is consistently reported by B2B advertisers who have run the experiment - splitting LinkedIn traffic between a generic product page and a page built or adapted specifically for that audience and offer. The lift is not driven by any single change. It is driven by the cumulative effect of removing every signal that tells the visitor \"this page was not made for you.\""}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"LinkedIn-specific pages differ from generic landing pages in four concrete ways. They reference the professional context the visitor came from - their role, their industry, the specific problem named in the ad. They use the same language register as the ad: precise, direct, peer-level. They lead with proof that is credible to that audience specifically - recognisable company logos, titles that match the target persona, metrics that mean something in that professional context. And they remove everything that would work fine for a general audience but creates friction for a professional buyer: consumer imagery, vague benefit language, and forms that do not signal professional intent."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"The mechanism is simple: reducing cognitive dissonance reduces exit rate. Every moment a professional visitor spends resolving a mismatch between the ad and the page is a moment they are not advancing toward conversion. LinkedIn-specific pages eliminate the dissonance at the source."}]]}]
1d:["$","section",null,{"className":"mt-6 rounded-md border border-border bg-bg-panel p-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold text-fg","children":"The Message Match Test for B2B"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"Before running LinkedIn traffic to any landing page, run it through these questions. Each \"no\" is a conversion leak."}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-4","children":[["$","li",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-fg","children":"Does the headline use the same vocabulary as the ad?"}]," ","Not a paraphrase - the same words, the same problem frame. A visitor who clicked on \"reduce engineering handoff time\" should not land on a page headed \"Streamline your workflow.\""]}],["$","li",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-fg","children":"Does the first screen contain proof specific to the target audience?"}]," ","Not generic five-star reviews. Case studies, logos, or metrics from companies and roles that match the LinkedIn audience you targeted."]}],["$","li",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-fg","children":"Is the language register consistent above the fold?"}]," ","Read the ad copy and then read the first 100 words of the landing page. Do they sound like they were written by the same person for the same reader?"]}],["$","li",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-fg","children":"Does the imagery match the professional context?"}]," ","No stock photos of generic office scenes. Product screenshots, customer logos, or context-specific visuals that signal \"this is built for a professional environment.\""]}],["$","li",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-fg","children":"Does the CTA match the intent of the LinkedIn click?"}]," ","Someone who clicked a thought leadership ad is not ready to \"Start a free trial.\" A soft CTA - \"See how it works for [role/industry]\" - matches the intent of a professional who is evaluating, not purchasing."]}],["$","li",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-fg","children":"Does the form ask for professional context?"}]," ","Work email, company size, or role - at least one signal that this offer is for professional buyers, not general consumers."]}]]}]]}]
1e:["$","section",null,{"className":"mt-6 rounded-md border border-border bg-bg-panel p-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold text-fg","children":"Quick Fixes: Adapting an Existing Page for LinkedIn Traffic"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"A full redesign is not always possible. These changes can be made to an existing page without touching the structure, and they address the most damaging mismatch signals first."}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-fg","children":"Rewrite the headline to mirror the ad"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"Take the core phrase or problem statement from the ad and make it the first thing the visitor reads on the page. This single change eliminates the most common source of register collapse - the visitor landed expecting one framing and received another."}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-fg","children":"Add a professional-context qualifier to the subheadline"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"A subheadline that names the target role or company type - \"Built for ops teams at scaling SaaS companies\" - immediately signals that the page was made for this specific visitor. It costs one line of copy and eliminates most of the \"is this for me?\" friction."}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-fg","children":"Move your most credible B2B proof above the fold"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"If you have logos from companies your LinkedIn audience recognises, or a case study result from a business at their scale, move it up. Even a single line - \"Trusted by ops teams at [Company A], [Company B], and [Company C]\" - positioned under the headline changes the credibility signal on arrival."}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-fg","children":"Replace the primary stock photo"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"Swap any generic office or lifestyle hero image for a product screenshot, a data visualisation, or a customer quote with a real name and title. This is often a 30-minute change and it removes one of the most visible credibility signals that the page was not built for a professional audience."}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-fg","children":"Change the form to require a work email"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"Add \"Work email\" as the field label rather than \"Email,\" and add client-side validation that flags common consumer domains. This is a 20-minute technical change that improves lead quality and signals to the visitor that this offer is professional-grade."}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-fg","children":"Soften the CTA to match evaluation intent"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"If your primary CTA is \"Start free trial\" or \"Buy now,\" add a secondary CTA that matches the intent of a LinkedIn professional who clicked to evaluate: \"See a 5-minute demo\" or \"Read how [Company] solved this.\" Most LinkedIn clicks come from people in an evaluation mindset, not a purchase mindset. A page that only offers a hard conversion CTA loses everyone in the evaluation phase - which on LinkedIn is the majority of your audience."}]]}]
1f:["$","section",null,{"className":"mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-accent/40 bg-bg-panel p-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-fg","children":"See What Your LinkedIn Visitors Actually Experience"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-6 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"Nebula audits your landing page against the LinkedIn ad driving traffic to it - checking register consistency, proof placement, CTA alignment, and form friction. You get a specific list of what to fix, ranked by priority."}],["$","$L19",null,{"href":"/#audit","className":"inline-block rounded bg-accent px-6 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-white transition-opacity hover:opacity-90","children":"Get Your Free LinkedIn Landing Page Audit"}]]}]
